  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 258 AM MDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nEastern Idaho remains positioned under broad southwesterly flow\nbetween an upper-level low moving through the Pacific Northwest\nand high pressure anchored over the South-Central U.S. The \nlatest convective outlook update has shifted the Slight Risk \narea south into Northern Utah, where low-level moisture and \ninstability are most favorably aligned. However, nearly all of \neastern Idaho remains under a Marginal Risk today. Effective \nbulk shear values exceeding 40 knots will remain sufficient to \norganize terrain-forced convection, making localized severe \ndownburst winds (50-60+ mph) and small hail the main threats \nwith stronger cores.\n\nHigh-resolution convective-allowing models (CAMs) are in strong\nagreement that shower and thunderstorm coverage will persist \nthroughout the day today and linger through tonight. Unlike \ntypical diurnal events that collapse quickly after sunset, \nelevated moisture and synoptic lift will keep precipitation \nactive into the early morning hours of Sunday.\n\nAs the primary upper trough axis pivots eastward across \nnorthern Montana and southern Alberta on Sunday, drier air will \nshunt into the region from the west. Showers and cloud cover \nwill linger through Sunday morning, particularly over the \nEastern Highlands, before a steady clearing trend takes hold \nfrom west to east during the afternoon hours. Temperatures on \nSunday will remain on the cool side of average, holding in the \nupper 60s and 70s across most valley locations.\n\nA transitional pattern takes shape for the new workweek as weak\nridging attempts to build into the Great Basin. Forecast\nguidance has nudged daytime temperatures slightly upward\ncompared to yesterday's model runs, pushing lower-elevation\nhighs back into the upper 70s to lower 80s through the week.\nHowever, operational models and ensemble clusters continue to\nexhibit significant spread regarding the long-range upper-level\npattern. Guidance is struggling to resolve how quickly secondary\nshortwave energy moving out of the Gulf of Alaska will flatten\nthe ridge or re-establish broad Pacific Northwest troughing.\nGiven this variance, a consensus blend remains the preferred\nforecast approach, keeping low-end, isolated afternoon mountain\nstorm chances and near-normal temperatures in place through the\nsecond half of next week.",
